Folio 70v has no independent derivation that I can read. The image is extremely faint, and every legible trace sits in the same position as an item on the preceding page (70r). This points to show-through or offset of the facing writing (or a very washed-out duplicate image), not a fresh layer of work.

The traces correspond to these items of 70r:
- top: zeta(s)=sum n^{-s} and Pi(s)zeta(s)=int_0^oo x^{s-1}/(e^x-1)dx;
- the contour-integral line Pi(s)zeta(s)e^{pi i s}2 sin(pi s)=int x^{s-1}/(e^x-1)dx, with the residues at x=2 pi i n leading to zeta(1-s);
- Prod(1-x^2/p^2);
- log zeta(s) = -sum_p log(1-p^{-s});
- zeta'/zeta(s) = -sum_p log p * p^{-s}/(1-p^{-s});
- the representation of log zeta via a prime-counting step function f(x);
- psi(s)=zeta(s)+zeta(2s)+... and psi(s)-psi(2s); the alternating chain -psi(3s)+psi(6s)-psi(5s)+psi(10s)+psi(15s)-psi(30s), i.e. Moebius inversion;
- the terms x^{1/n-1}/log x and the integrals of dx/log x between a^{1/n} and b^{1/n};
- d log Pi(x)/dx = psi(x), psi(x)=1/x+psi(x-1), i.e. the digamma recurrence;
- the partial sum psi(n,s)=sum_1^n k^{-s} with its integral representation;
- at the bottom: log p * s = 2 pi i n, hence x = p^{1/n}.

The last item is the poles/periodicity of 1/(1-p^{-s}) at s=2 pi i n/log p, which links the primes p to the prime powers p^{1/n}.

Mathematically, the content is the ingredient list of Riemann's 1859 paper:
- Gamma integral for zeta;
- loop integral and functional equation;
- Euler product and log zeta;
- Moebius inversion from Pi(x)=sum pi(x^{1/n})/n to pi(x) via li(x^{1/n}).

These are all reconstructed from 70r, since nothing beyond it is legible here. No new step can be attributed to 70v itself.
